# Reference template. The live Caddyfile is generated by scripts/install.sh.
#
# Domain mode uses DNS-01 wildcard TLS exclusively. Caddy issues exactly two
# certificates — $DOMAIN and *.$DOMAIN — covering every present and future
# sandbox subdomain, sidestepping Let's Encrypt's 50-cert/week-per-registered-
# domain ceiling. HTTP-01 on-demand was removed because the per-subdomain ask
# endpoint allowed arbitrary subdomain probes to burn the issuance quota.
#
# Optional shared cert storage (opt-in, see setup/multi-node-cert-sharing.md):
# when scripts/install.sh is invoked with --caddy-storage-s3, an additional
# `storage s3 { ... }` directive is injected into the global block below.
# certmagic-s3 then holds a distributed lock during ACME, so exactly one node
# issues / renews the wildcard while the rest read it from S3 — the path that
# matters once a cluster has 10+ ingress nodes racing for the same wildcard.
#
# Substitution placeholders below:
#   {{ROOT_SITE_ADDRESS}}     — the apex domain ($DOMAIN)
#   {{WILDCARD_SITE_ADDRESS}} — *.$DOMAIN
#   {{DNS_PROVIDER}}          — DNS provider name passed to --dns-provider
#
# Two opt-in variants scripts/install.sh writes (plans/identity-and-access.md
# §5.11); a default install writes neither:
#
#   --l4-proxy-protocol: caddy-l4's SNI fallback sends a PROXY v2 header to
#     the HTTPS sites (SB_L4_FALLBACK_PROXY_PROTOCOL=true), so the global
#     block gains
#         servers 127.0.0.1:8443 {
#             listener_wrappers {
#                 proxy_protocol {
#                     allow 127.0.0.1/32
#                 }
#                 tls
#             }
#         }
#     and the API reverse_proxy block gains
#         header_up X-AerolVM-Client-IP {remote_host}
#     Write the wrapper before setting the flag, and unset the flag before
#     removing the wrapper: the wrapper also accepts a connection with no
#     header, while the flag without the wrapper fails every handshake.
#
#   --pat-scope host: the operator token works only from the host and
#     cluster peers (SB_PAT_SCOPE=host), so the API reverse_proxy moves from
#     127.0.0.1:21212 to sandboxd's public socket, where every request is
#     public:
#         reverse_proxy unix//run/sandboxd-public/api.sock {
#     Any other reverse proxy on the host must use the socket too.
#
# One more opt-in variant, for a node behind a health-checked load balancer
# (Terraform api_load_balancer = "nlb"):
#
#   --lb-health-port <port>: a plain-HTTP site the balancer's health check
#     probes. GET /health walks the path a client takes (caddy-l4 on :443,
#     its SNI fallback, the HTTPS API site, sandboxd), so it answers 502
#     while any of them is down and the balancer stops sending the node new
#     connections. The probe carries neither SNI nor the domain as Host, so
#     the site supplies both; without the Host header the probe matches no
#     site and Caddy answers an empty 200 even with sandboxd down.
#         http://:<port> {
#             handle /health {
#                 reverse_proxy https://127.0.0.1:443 {
#                     header_up Host {{ROOT_SITE_ADDRESS}}
#                     stream_close_delay 10m
#                     transport http {
#                         tls_server_name {{ROOT_SITE_ADDRESS}}
#                         tls_insecure_skip_verify
#                     }
#                 }
#             }
#             handle {
#                 respond 404
#             }
#         }
#     Keep it unbound (":<port>", not "<ip>:<port>"): Caddy names servers in
#     sorted listen-address order, and the API site must stay srv0, the
#     server sandboxd writes its routes into. Open the port to the balancer
#     only.

{
    admin localhost:2019
}

https://{{ROOT_SITE_ADDRESS}} {
    tls {
        dns {{DNS_PROVIDER}} {env.SB_DNS_API_TOKEN}
    }
    @api path /health /v1 /v1/* /daytona /daytona/* /e2b /e2b/* /runloop /runloop/* /mcp /ui /ui/* /.well-known/aerolvm-auth /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource*
    handle @api {
        # stream_close_delay: a Caddy reload closes each WebSocket this proxy
        # carries (exec streams, aerolvm shell, session attach) with code
        # 1001 unless a delay is set (#712). A stream that outlives a
        # reload keeps the old config in memory until it closes or the delay
        # ends.
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:21212 {
            stream_close_delay 10m
        }
    }
    handle {
        respond "Sandbox API not found" 404
    }
}

https://{{WILDCARD_SITE_ADDRESS}} {
    tls {
        dns {{DNS_PROVIDER}} {env.SB_DNS_API_TOKEN}
    }
    # close: caddy-l4 picks a backend once per TCP connection; a client that
    # connected before its route existed must reconnect to be routed again.
    respond "Sandbox not found" 404 {
        close
    }
}
